## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable, isolated QoS for multi-tenant CNFs with real-time config updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hierarchical token bucket, per-tenant and per-path
- Dynamic priority scheduling and cross-tenant isolation
- Fast policy propagation and SLA metrics
- Kubernetes manifests and minimal tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo token bucket checker
class TB {
  constructor(cap, ref) { this.cap = cap; this.refill = ref; }
  consume(n=1){ if(this.cap>=n){ this.cap-=n; return true;} return false; }
  refillAt(now){ this.cap = Math.min(this.max, this.cap + (now-this.last)*this.fillRate); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test SLA adherence under bursty traffic?
- How would you roll out policy changes safely across clusters?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-tenant routing through ConfigMaps, path rewriting, and versioning with safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Namespace-scoped ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Request rewriting via tenant header
- Versioning strategy and default fallback
- Minimal YAML manifests for end-to-end validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo rewrite function
function rewrite(req, tenantId, version, mapping) {
  const base = mapping[tenantId] || mapping['default'];
  req.url = `/v${version || '1'}/api${req.path}`;
  req.headers['Accept'] = 'application/json';
  req.headers['Host'] = base.host;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test unknown tenants and version upgrades?
- How to handle partial mappings and rollback?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to securely manage per-tenant TLS credentials in a CNF gateway, including secret-driven config, hot-reload of TLS material, and per-tenant mTLS verification.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes Secrets mounted as TLS material for sidecars
- Hot-reload of TLS credentials without pod restart
- Mutual TLS with per-tenant CA certificates
- Rotation cadence, error handling, and tenant isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo example: load TLS certs from mounted files and reload on change
let tlsContext = tls.createSecureContext({
  ca: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/ca.pem'),
  cert: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/cert.pem'),
  key: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/key.pem')
});

// watch secret mount and refresh TLS context
fs.watch('/mnt/cnf-tls', () => {
  tlsContext = tls.createSecureContext({
    ca: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/ca.pem'),
    cert: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/cert.pem'),
    key: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/key.pem')
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate certificate expiry and revocation in this flow?
- What are the trade-offs between in-cluster secret rotation vs external secret management (e.g., Vault) for CNF gateways?

## Why This Is Asked
Auditing and non-repudiation in CNF gateways are critical for multi-tenant environments. This question validates practical expertise with Kubernetes Secrets, HMAC signing, sidecar log pipelines, and retention configuration—essential skills for implementing post-deployment security controls.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped auditing and non-repudiation
- HMAC signing using per-tenant Kubernetes Secrets
- Sidecar-based log forwarding to in-cluster sinks
- ConfigMap-driven log retention and namespace scoping

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');
function signLog(logLine, secret) {
  return crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret)
    .update(logLine)
    .digest('hex');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical end-to-end config provenance and policy enforcement for multi-tenant CNF platforms, a common production risk area beyond image provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Config payload signing with Cosign and KMS-backed keys
- SBOM attachment for config changes
- Admission control with Kyverno/OPA enforcing signature, SBOM, and tenant labels
- Canary namespace validation before rollout
- Per-tenant rollback mechanisms and status surfacing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo workflow: verify config update integrity before applying
async function verifyConfigUpdate(payload, signature, sbom) {
  const okSig = await verifySignature(payload, signature);
  const okSBOM = await validateSBOM(payload, sbom);
  const tenantOk = checkTenantLabel(payload);
  return okSig && okSBOM && tenantOk;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback semantics across tenants?
- How would you handle SBOM drift when multiple tenants update concurrently?
